Upon application of transverse compression (i.e., normal to the plane of the layers), the structure undergoes densification. This results in a progressive compenetrating of neighboring layers, leading to a nonlinear increase in the number of physical contact points. Crucially, as the compression proceeds beyond the limit of interlayer spacing, non-adjacent layers begin to interpenetrate (for example, in Fig.  S5 , a new interface I 1-3  is formed between layer 1 and layer 3, initially separated by layer 2). This layer’s interpenetration generates new contact events that were previously geometrically forbidden. The model treats the fibers as deformable, randomly oriented cylinders within each layer, and describes the layer interpenetration as a function of the reduction of thickness due to the applied compression. Contact evolution is estimated using a percolation-inspired approach, in which the number of effective fiber–fiber contacts scales with the degree of interlayer mixing. The formation of new contacts is considered a function of the overlap integral between neighboring and non-neighboring layers, such that the total number of contacts n i  after the i-th interpenetration can be expressed as the product of the number of contacts  d l  in a single interface between layers times the number of interfaces N i  in the material. The number of contacts at the interface  d l  is considered constant as a parameter resulting from the fabrication process, while the number of interfaces N i  increases proportionally as the material is compressed, following: 6 N i = i + 1 T 0 - ∑ a = 0 i a + 1 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
